{
  "id": "cousins-in-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "cousins-in-binary-tree",
  "slug": "cousins-in-binary-tree",
  "title": "Cousins in Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you need both the depth and the parent of x and y - can one level-by-level pass collect both at once?",
    "as you BFS, push (child index, parent index) pairs onto the queue instead of just indices, so you always know each node's parent",
    "once you find x's depth and parent, and separately y's depth and parent, cousins just means depths match and parents don't",
    "you can even short-circuit: once both x and y have been found, stop the BFS early"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "checking only parent inequality and forgetting to also require equal depth",
    "treating the root's absence of a parent incorrectly, causing a crash or wrong comparison when one target is the root",
    "comparing node indices instead of parent identity when two different parents happen to hold equal values (values are unique here, but the logic should still key off parent position, not value)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a\ncomplete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child (sentinel) but does have a right child, 4.\n\nTwo nodes are \"cousins\" if they sit at the same depth in the tree but have\ndifferent parents (so siblings, which share a parent, are not cousins).\nYou're given two node values, `x` and `y`, that are guaranteed to both\nexist in the tree and to be distinct from each other.\n\n**Determine whether the nodes holding values `x` and `y` are cousins.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "x and y are guaranteed to exist in the tree and are distinct values"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "x and y are siblings (same parent) - answer is false",
      "x and y are at different depths entirely - answer is false",
      "one of x or y is the root - the root has no siblings or cousins, so the answer is false"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, -101, 4, -101, 5], x = 4, y = 5",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "4 is 2's right child (depth 2, parent 2) and 5 is 3's right child (depth 2, parent 3) - same depth, different parents"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, -101, -101], x = 4, y = 5",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "4 and 5 are both children of node 2 - same parent, so they are siblings, not cousins"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, -101, 5, -101], x = 4, y = 5",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "4 is 2's left child and 5 is 3's left child - same depth, different parents"
      }
    ],
